    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Pinochle Three Player',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Three-player Pinochle: each plays for themselves. Deal 15 cards each and a 3-card kitty. Auction to name trump (min 20, or 19 for dealer); high bidder takes the kitty, declares trump, and all meld. Fifteen tricks; follow suit, trump when void. First to 100 points wins. Must take at least one trick to count meld; fail contract costs the bid amount.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Pinochle descends from the European game Bezique, either as a cousin of Binokel or directly from the Swiss and South German game Binocle (Binokel), which is itself a descendant of Bezique. German immigrants brought Binokel to America in the last quarter of the 19th century, where the name was mispronounced and misspelled as pinochle. The game became a favorite among American Jewish and Irish immigrants. The three-player auction variant developed in the United States; its bidding has similarities to German Skat and to bid whist. During World War I the game was briefly outlawed in Syracuse, New York, and some other US cities due to anti-German sentiment, but it has since regained broad popularity.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Three players; each plays for themselves.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '48-card Pinochle deck (two copies of 9 through Ace in each of four suits).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards only.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Dealer deals 15 cards to each player and 3 cards to the kitty (middle). Deal rotates clockwise; packets of three or four at a time, starting with player to dealer's left.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Cutthroat: No kitty. Deal entire deck: 16 cards each. Win only by bidding and making contract to reach winning score; high score without a winning bid does not win.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Check: Gambling variant; same as to 100 but players track checks (e.g. stakes). Flush/run, aces, roundtable earn checks; looking at talon and losing costs checks; double marriage, double pinochle, double arounds earn extra.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
